  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nShowers continue to taper in coverage across the area this\nafternoon in the wake of expansive composite outflow racing\nsouthward toward southern Illinois and Missouri. Gradual\nclearing of clouds has commenced allowing for temperatures to \nfinally begin rising this afternoon. The expectation remains \nfor highs to reach the mid to upper 70s before gradually falling\nafter sunset. A few sprinkles may continue from time to time \nsouth of I-80 and especially US-24 through the evening. \n\nAnother shortwave over the central Rockies is expected to shear\nrapidly eastward into the lower Missouri River Valley later \ntonight and into tomorrow likely resulting in an uptick in \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms over Missouri. As this \nactivity pushes east into Illinois, it is possible that the \nnorthern flanks of the showers could scrape across our far \nsouthern CWA late tonight into Thursday morning but the vast \nmajority of this rain and all of the associated thunderstorm \nactivity should stay south of our area. \n\nBy Thursday night, the center of the high pressure ridge should\nset up over the area. This should set the stage for nearly \nideal radiational cooling with clear skies and calm winds. Have \nnudged low temps downward Thursday night, especially in the \ntypical cool spots where lows could dip into the 40s. The urban \nheat island should keep the heat of the city of Chicago in the \nlower 60s for low temps. Can envision areas of shallow fog\ndeveloping by daybreak, but will withhold mention in the\nforecast for now. Friday looks like a spectacular early autumn \nday with highs around 80, low humidity, and sunny skies.\n\nThe weekend forecast overall looks ok, though forecast \nconfidence is a bit lower with some chance it could rain a bit \nat some point. Another trough is expected to pass well north of \nthe area over the weekend with a weakening cold front reaching \nthe area Saturday or Saturday night before probably washing out \naltogether. Cannot totally rule out a few showers with that \nfront, but given weak frontal convergence, antecedent dry air \nmass, and minimal upper forcing, the more likely scenario looks \nto be dry weather for most. Southerly winds in advance of this \nfront should allow temps to warm more solidly into the 80s on \nSaturday. Another trough is expected to move into the region \nearly next week bringing what looks to be a somewhat better \nchance of showers and thunderstorms in the Monday/Tuesday time \nframe. There is still model variability in the timing and track \nof that trough, so forecast confidence is still on the lower end\nof the spectrum.\n\n-Izzi / Borchardt",
